#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088609} :activity/id #uuid "bc935836-c5f6-5bf6-8105-6072d1f0946d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049719 :topic/id #uuid "0df888e0-a91f-54c6-bf91-7bc542e0c3e4" :topic/math-academy-id 867} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088609 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088930 :tutorial/content "A **dilation** of scale factor $k$ with the center at the origin is a linear transformation that multiplies the standard basis vectors by $k.$ It is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFor example, let \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n be the matrix of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}.$ This transformation is a dilation of scale factor $2.$\n\nTo visualize the dilation $\\mathbf{T},$ consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square $\\mathrm{R}$ spanned by the vectors, as shown below. \n\n![](images/6e/6e38185922e9cad6d3961e7c5e755eabf4c52eafd1cc81a1e81a383b4df5ae82.png)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =Ti=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=2i, \\\\ {j}^{'} & =Tj=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ multiplies vectors $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ by $2.$ The resulting vectors span a larger region, shown below.\n\n![](images/cc/cc6677c641a2a713e0324db90edfdd86a5ca1c096c0ffd70f1216119f36da645.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "689fa0a7-c69e-5bd3-8a10-31aca7d9871f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3536 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b3147c60-fe22-5922-b00c-9f4e25a56724" :step/math-academy-id 20028 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088612}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088610 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088931 :tutorial/content "A **reflection in the $x$-axis** is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo visualize a reflection in the $x$-axis, consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square $\\mathrm{R}$ spanned by the vectors, as shown below. \n\n![](images/9e/9e322e6ed1ddb8f1d094633e931cd179b7eb9d6f47acd7c7d0809f113df91b22.png)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/b9/b93bb91dae85a3e3b40a8d0ade3d4f420064aaa6926431fa7f664c977bb8fd59.png)\n\nSimilarly, a **reflection in the $y$-axis** is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAgain, we can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/49/4993d8586a84e4a40a54edddd8011cd3d516264bfffc7e3c33d27a10edf1e7d7.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "f38c3a51-d627-5de8-aa0b-e8e8064003c9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3537 :tutorial/title "Reflection in the X and Y Axes"} :step/id #uuid "3318223f-ba60-5669-a52f-d290b463770d" :step/math-academy-id 20033 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088665}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087751 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087567 :question/id #uuid "8559e2bf-8b48-5e26-81c6-0db03d93645a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5451"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf99414e-f1cf-528f-adbb-407cf6e82c4c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050103}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401137}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Linear Transformation Represented by a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "06bf3e6f-207a-50aa-b32c-11f8a634e387" :step/math-academy-id 20034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087752 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087568 :question/id #uuid "ef17b637-7c4d-5515-99b7-13b1290244f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5452"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "166cd7e4-5568-5769-8bc6-4ffc4ed8b508" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050136}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393821}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Matrix of a Dilation Given a Vector and its Image"} :step/id #uuid "6cfaedb9-ffad-54bf-a47e-87c0ea841b5a" :step/math-academy-id 20035 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088610}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088665 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088956 :tutorial/content "A **reflection in line $y=x$** is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/0e/0e37a258ea833a2d490fc34a34af40a5c7a18f7589f107c6d0ed631b331c8f2a.png)\n\nSimilarly, a **reflection in line $y=-x$** is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/4a/4a83673a409fd2e5d33b0b5f743f57af08cfb27c38e312a636179102c2172b04.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "9b34a369-035e-5610-a337-6169ea364331" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4161 :tutorial/title "Reflection in the Lines y=x and y=-x"} :step/id #uuid "0aa5472d-88af-57aa-8bf3-c32dc3b082a4" :step/math-academy-id 25326 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088611}}] :activity/title "Dilations and Reflections as Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087934}]}
